 "overview": {
  "summary": "The precision half of the ac voltage function. A13 makes an ac sine wave of roughly the right amplitude; A12 decides what the amplitude should be and holds it there. It does that with two nested loops. The fast one is analog: an averaging converter rectifies the sensed output, an error integrator compares that current against the dc reference current from the DAC assembly (A11), and the result leaves as OSC CONT to drive A13's agc amplifier — quick load-regulation recovery and short-term stability, but not accurate enough on its own. The accurate one is thermal and runs under software: the same signal is applied alternately as ac and as dc to a Fluke thermal RMS sensor (U14), the two readings are compared through the RCL line, and a 15-bit dac trims the reference current until the ac/dc difference is zero. A second thermal sensor (U16) characterises the frequency response of the first. Everything else on the board — nine latching relays, an 82C55 and two driver ICs, a CMOS analog switch, a diagnostic multiplexer — exists to steer those two loops between ranges and frequencies. A12 also provides the amplitude control for the ac current function and, at low frequencies, for the Wideband AC Module (Option -03).",
  "sections": "§2-119 … §2-129",
  "note": "A12 generates no supply of its own except +5 OSC, which U25 regulates from +15 OSC. The ±15 OSC pair arrives on P501 1A/1C and 2A/2C with OSC COM on 3A/3C; ±44 S, ±17 S and SCOM arrive on P502 28A/28C, 29A/29C, 30A/30C, 31A/31C and 32A/32C; +5 LH and LH COM feed the digital section. L3/L4 with C34/C35 buffer ±15 OSC into the ±15A pair and L1/L2 with C86/C87 into the ±15B pair, so a fault that looks like one amplifier group misbehaving may be one of those four chokes. Two divider-derived references matter as much as the rails: +2.5 V from +5 LH through R52/R53, and −200 mV from −15 OSC through R57/R58, which exists only to bias the thermal-sensor protection comparators."
 },
