  {
   "title": "The locator page's own caption says it is a continuation, and it is not",
   "text": "Chapter 7 PDF page 55 is the A12 component locator, drawing 5700A-1650, and the schematic sheets follow it on pages 56, 57 and 58 as 5700A-1050 (1 of 3), (2 of 3) and (3 of 3). But the printed caption on page 55 reads 'Figure 7-14. A12 Oscillator Control PCA (cont)' even though it is the first page of the figure — all four pages carry the '(cont)' form. The title-block drawing number is what settles it: 16xx is a component locator, 10xx is a schematic sheet. Page 59 is already A13's locator, 5700A-1651.",
   "refs": []
  },
  {
   "title": "U8 and U19 are the same device type on the board, and the 1996 parts list has them swapped",
   "text": "Table 6-15 in the 1996 manual gives U8 as 'IC,ANALOG SWITCH,DG211,+-15V,175 OHMS,QUAD BILATERAL,DIP16', stock 586735, and U19 as 'CMOS DG444', stock 910708. The Rev 9 parts list groups both under one row, stock 910708, quad bilateral switch. Figure 7-14 annotates U8B and U8C 'DG444' on sheet 1 and U19A–U19D 'DG211' on sheet 2 — the opposite way round from the 1996 list. The photographed board settles it in practice: both U8 and U19 are marked DG444DJ. So the two are interchangeable in the sense that matters for a repair, but do not order a DG211 for U8 on the strength of the 1996 list without looking at the fitted part first. Note that both schematic annotations print 'ON= <175', which is the DG211's on-resistance figure, not the DG444's.",
   "refs": [
    "U8",
    "U19"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "R50 is 47 Ω, not the 120 Ω the Rev 9 parts list gives it",
   "text": "The Rev 9 list prints one row 'R 32, 50  RES,CF,120,+-5%,0.25W'. Every other source says otherwise: the 1996 list gives R50 its own row at RES,CF,47, Figure 7-14 sheet 2 annotates the part '47' beside the K6 coil, and the photographed board's R50 carries yellow-violet-black bands. R32 really is 120 Ω. The as-built row shown against R50 is the Rev 9 row and is reproduced as printed, but treat 47 Ω as the value.",
   "refs": [
    "R50",
    "R32"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "U14 and U16 are described as two completely different parts by the two lists",
   "text": "The Rev 9 list calls stock 842591 'RMS CONVERTER TESTED 400 OHM-A GRADE'. The 1996 list replaces it with stock 1577442, 'OP AMP LT1220CST ON 12 PIN ADAPTER BOARD', and adds note 1 — 'U14, U16, part number 1577442 are assemblies'. Chapter 2 and §5-17 both describe the part in circuit as a thermal RMS sensor (§5-17 step 9 calls TP7 'the output of the Fluke RMS Sensor'), which is the Rev 9 description. The most likely reading is that the later part is a replacement module built around an LT1220 that plugs into the same twelve-pin footprint, but the manuals nowhere say so. Both are recorded; check what is actually fitted before ordering.",
   "refs": [
    "U14",
    "U16"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "R55 appears twice in the 1996 parts list",
   "text": "Table 6-15 lists R55 once in the group 'R1,R2,R55  RES,CF,30K,+-5%,0.25W' (quantity 3) and again on its own as 'R55  RESISTOR A52R,RES,CF,30K,+-5%,0.25W' (quantity 1). Both rows carry the same stock number, 368753, and the same value, so nothing is ambiguous about the part — but the two rows together imply four pieces for three designators. The Rev 9 list has only the grouped row. The dataset carries the second row's description, which is the more specific of the two.",
   "refs": [
    "R55",
    "R1",
    "R2"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Most of A12's resistors were re-sourced between the two revisions",
   "text": "Twenty-four rows of Table 6-15 fail to join between the Rev 9 and 1996 lists on stock number while agreeing exactly on value, tolerance and rating — the whole carbon-film and metal-film population, plus CR6–CR8, Q1, Q2, VR1–VR3 and Z5. These are a change of supplier rather than a change of part, and they are why so many components on this board carry an 'as built' row that says the same thing twice with a different number. The one substantive difference in that set is Z5: 10 kΩ ±2% in the 1996 list, ±5% in Rev 9.",
   "refs": [
    "Z5"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "§5-17 step 4's oscilloscope settings and its own figure disagree",
   "text": "Step 4 says to set the oscilloscope to 2 V/div and 200 µs/div for the waveform at the anode of CR4, and step 6 says 500 mV/div and 200 µs/div for the one at U11 pin 6. Both figures (5-27, 5-28) are annotated 1 V and 10 ms. At the 1 kHz the procedure calls for, 200 µs/div is the setting that shows the waveform the figures draw; the figure annotations look like reused artwork. Use the step's settings.",
   "refs": [
    "CR4",
    "U11"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "The board has no test point on any supply rail",
   "text": "All eight test points are signal points. Nothing on A12 brings out +15 OSC, −15 OSC, ±15A, ±15B, +5 OSC, ±17 S, ±44 S or +5 LH at a loop, and Chapter 2 tabulates no supply for this assembly, so there is no published nominal for any of them here. Measure them at the P501 and P502 pins listed in the overview, against the return each belongs to, and use Table 2-10 (A17's regulated outputs) for what they should be — they are A17's supplies, not A12's.",
   "refs": [
    "P501",
    "P502",
    "U25"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "The manufacturer columns are OCR of a scan, and some of them are visibly wrong",
   "text": "The manufacturer supply code and manufacturer part number come from the scanned Rev 9 list and are carried through as read. Several are mangled — HS1's part number reads 'j2zees', R59/R60's reads 'MFSS5SD4991F' for what is plainly MF55D4991F, VR3's reads 'AN751A' for 1N751A — and VR1/VR2's '1N748' is a 3.9 V device where the description on the same row says 10.0 V, so it is more likely a misread of 1N758. Treat the Fluke stock number, which is a single isolated six- or seven-digit field and survives OCR cleanly, as the reliable key.",
   "refs": [
    "HS1",
    "R59",
    "R60",
    "VR1",
    "VR2",
    "VR3"
   ]
  }
